mn n (maybe none for two lines). But we can also have lines that cover only points in column n-2 and n-1, etc. The requirement is that each line is not vertical/horizontal/slope -1. So we need to find three lines with distinct slopes (or at least not those slopes) that pass through the required points.\n\nOne possible construction: Use lines:\n\n- L1: through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1. That's slope 1, which is sunny (since not 0,∞,-1). This line also passes through (n,3) which is not a point we need. So L1 covers (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Good.\n\n- L2: through (n-2,2) and (n,1). Slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = -1/2 = -0.5, which is not -1,0,∞, so sunny. This line also passes through (n-1,1.5) which is not integer point. So L2 covers (n-2,2) and (n,1).\n\n- L3: through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). Slope = (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, which is sunny. This line also passes through (n,? ) maybe (n, -1) not needed. So L3 covers (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). So all six points are covered by three sunny lines. So we have a construction with exactly three sunny lines, plus vertical lines for columns 1..n-3 (n-3 lines). That's total n lines, exactly three sunny lines. So k=3 is possible.\n\nNow k≥4: The solution says impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. Let's examine: If we have at least 4 sunny lines, we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. The vertical lines can cover entire columns. Horizontal lines can cover at most one point per column. Slope -1 lines also at most one per column. So to cover all points, we need to cover each column's points. The columns 1..n-3 have many points (≥4). If we have only n-4 non-sunny lines, we can at most have n-4 vertical lines (if we use all non-sunny lines as vertical). That would cover at most n-4 columns fully. But we have n columns total. However, we have 4 sunny lines that can each cover at most one point per column. So they can collectively cover at